    "result_text": "The build is green. Only the acceptance test suite exists in this project, and it passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the GOLD membership tier rule following the existing codebase pattern (where `membershipTier` is derived in the MapStruct mapper from persisted `Owner` fields like `namesakeCount`).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `household_size` column with getter/setter, mirroring the existing `namesakeCount`/`bulkSignupWarning` fields.\n\n2. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 added the `household_size` column. The test profile uses `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in the schema.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 at create time, computes the household size (existing owners sharing the same normalized last name + address, plus this new owner) and stores it. This captures the count *after this create*, using the same household-key normalization that produces `householdId`.\n\n4. **`OwnerMapper.membershipTier`** \u2014 returns `\"GOLD\"` when `householdSize >= 3`; otherwise the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules apply.\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipTier` field description for documentation consistency.\n\nThe rule composes correctly with existing logic: the third household member (same last name + address, `sharesHousehold: true`) is stored with `householdSize = 3` and reads back as `GOLD`, while the earlier members and all other owners keep their existing tiers.",
